window.docContents[104].resolve({"/#literal-numbers":{"contents":"It is quite inconvenient to write out a sequence of constructors for positive numbers.\nOne way to work around the problem would be to provide a function to convert a  into a .\nHowever, this approach has downsides.\nFirst off, because  cannot represent , the resulting function would either convert a  to a bigger number, or it would return .\nNeither is particularly convenient for users.\nSecondly, the need to call the function explicitly would make programs that use positive numbers much less convenient to write than programs that use .\nHaving a trade-off between precise types and convenient APIs means that the precise types become less useful.\n\nThere are three type classes that are used to overload numeric literals: , , and .\nBecause many types have values that are naturally written with , the  class allow these specific values to be overridden.\nIt is defined as follows:\n\n\n\nBecause  is not a positive number, there should be no instance of .\n\nSimilarly, many types have values that are naturally written with .\nThe  class allows these to be overridden:\n\n\n\nAn instance of  makes perfect sense:\n\n\n\nWith this instance,  can be used for :\n\n\n\n\n\nIn Lean, natural number literals are interpreted using a type class called :\n\n\n\nThis type class takes two arguments:  is the type for which a natural number is overloaded, and the unnamed  argument is the actual literal number that was encountered in the program.\nThe method  is then used as the value of the numeric literal.\nBecause the class contains the  argument, it becomes possible to define only instances for those values where the number makes sense.\n\n demonstrates that the arguments to type classes do not need to be types.\nBecause types in Lean are first-class participants in the language that can be passed as arguments to functions and given definitions with def and abbrev, there is no barrier that prevents non-type arguments in positions where a less-flexible language could not permit them.\nThis flexibility allows overloaded operations to be provided for particular values as well as particular types.\nAdditionally, it allows the Lean standard library to arrange for there to be a  instance whenever there's an  instance, and vice versa.\nSimilarly, an instance of  implies an instance of , just as an instance of  implies an instance of .\n\nA sum type that represents natural numbers less than four can be defined as follows:\n\n\n\nWhile it would not make sense to allow any literal number to be used for this type, numbers less than four clearly make sense:\n\n\n\nWith these instances, the following examples work:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn the other hand, out-of-bounds literals are still not allowed:\n\n\n\n\n\nFor , the  instance should work for any  other than .\nAnother way to phrase this is to say that for all natural numbers , the instance should work for .\nJust as names like  automatically become implicit arguments to functions that Lean fills out on its own, instances can take automatic implicit arguments.\nIn this instance, the argument  stands for any , and the instance is defined for a  that's one greater:\n\n\n\nBecause  stands for a  that's one less than what the user wrote, the helper function  returns a  that's one greater than its argument.\nThis makes it possible to use natural number literals for positive numbers, but not for zero:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Positive Numbers","header":"3.1.5. Literal Numbers","id":"/#literal-numbers"}});